    "answer": {
      "short": "Write $P(x)=(x-a)(x-b)Q(x)+Ax+B$. Substituting $x=a$ and $x=b$ gives $P(a)=Aa+B$ and $P(b)=Ab+B$; solve these simultaneously to obtain the stated formulas.",
      "worked": "Since the divisor is quadratic, write\n$$P(x)=(x-a)(x-b)Q(x)+Ax+B.$$\nSubstitute $x=a$ and $x=b$:\n$$\\begin{align*}P(a)&=Aa+B\\\\P(b)&=Ab+B\\\\P(a)-P(b)&=A(a-b)\\\\A&=\\frac{P(a)-P(b)}{a-b}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThen\n$$\\begin{align*}B&=P(a)-aA\\\\B&=P(a)-\\frac{a(P(a)-P(b))}{a-b}\\\\B&=\\frac{(a-b)P(a)-aP(a)+aP(b)}{a-b}\\\\B&=\\frac{aP(b)-bP(a)}{a-b}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe divisions are valid because $a\\ne b$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
